From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cristian Papa <pcristian292@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 00:37:45 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] net: airoha: xpon: reject an OMCI GEM port beyond the GEM table

airoha_gpon_omci_hw_set_gem_port() takes the Port-ID of a GEM port
network CTP as the OLT wrote it, a 16-bit value. Since 999-68 a delete
(valid = false) goes on to clear the port's bit in omci_gems even when
the table write failed, and the write fails with -EINVAL for a Port-ID
of 4096 or more: the bit was then cleared up to 8 KB past the 4096-bit
map, in struct xpon_priv or after it.

The agent does not store a CTP with such a Port-ID, because the create
or set that would do so calls this function with valid = true first and
is refused. That check lives in another module and is easy to lose:
refuse the Port-ID here, before anything indexes a bitmap with it. The
value returned for it does not change.

Found in a security review of the GPON code.

Signed-off-by: Cristian Papa <pcristian292@gmail.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Mythos 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_xpon.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_xpon.c
@@ -2862,6 +2862,15 @@ int airoha_gpon_omci_hw_set_gem_port(voi
 	int ret;
 
 	/*
+	 * The Port-ID is the 16-bit value the OLT wrote in the GEM port
+	 * network CTP. Nothing below may index the GEM bitmaps with one the
+	 * GEM table cannot hold, also when a delete goes on after a failed
+	 * table write.
+	 */
+	if (gem_port_id >= GPON_MAX_GEM_ID)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	/*
 	 * The OLT may announce encryption with Encrypted_Port-ID before OMCI
 	 * creates the GEM port, as Nokia/ALCL OLTs do. Keep it when OMCI then
 	 * writes the port, as the vendor stack does, or the port is left
